    "icon": "data:image/png;base64,",
    "description": "Classifies items into A, B and C by cumulative share of value (Pareto), optionally within groups, plus XYZ demand-variability classes for ABC-XYZ inventory analysis.",
    "category": "Analytics",
    "subcategory": "Supply Chain",
    "tags": [
        "abc analysis",
        "pareto",
        "xyz",
        "inventory",
        "classification",
        "80/20",
        "supply chain",
        "visokio-agents"
    ],
    "introductoryText": "The Pareto principle as a block: rank items (products, customers, suppliers) by value and classify them by **cumulative share of the total**:\n\n- **A**: the items that together make the first 80% of value (usually a small share of items)\n- **B**: the next 15% (up to 95%)\n- **C**: the remaining 5% (usually most items)\n\nThresholds are options, and classes can be computed **within groups** (per warehouse, category or region). Sales lines are aggregated per item first, so you can feed transactions directly.\n\nAdd a **date field** and the block also performs **XYZ analysis**: per item, the coefficient of variation of demand across weeks, months or quarters (periods with no sales count as zero). **X** = steady (CV up to 0.5), **Y** = variable (up to 1.0), **Z** = erratic. Combined classes such as *AX* (high value, steady: automate replenishment) or *CZ* (low value, erratic: consider make-to-order or delisting) are the basis of many inventory policies.\n\nThe second output summarises each class: items, value, shares and a suggested policy.",
    "dependencies": "",
